  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe introduce a multi-resolution optimization with step-function sequences (constant over intervals) to minimize the AC1 bound. We start from a coarse resolution (large grid-spacing) and iteratively refine the solution (smaller grid-spacing) to capture detailed features. This approach combines global search with local refinement through:\n\n- Periodic upsampling of the current best solution\n- Grid-based initialization of new populations\n- Simulated binary crossover genetic algorithm\n- Repair mechanisms to ensure the sequence sum meets validator requirements\n\nThe rationale is twofold: first, step-function sequences are effective for many optimization problems, often yielding simple, interpretable solutions. Second, starting in coarse resolution allows escaping local minima, while progressive upscaling allows meticulous adjustment.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe sequence \\(a\\) is represented by heights \\(h_0, h_1, \\ldots\\) and grid breakpoints. For a grid fraction \\(f\\), the grid-points are:\n  \\[grid = \\text{sorted}( \\{ k \\Delta \\mid k=0,\\ldots,K \\} )\\]\nwhere \\(\\Delta = 1/f\\) (upsampled factorial). The resulting sequence's values alternate between consecutive heights at the breaker points. \n\nConstraints are handled by repair mechanisms. The objective score is:\n  \\[\\text{score} = 2 \\times n \\times \\max(\\text{convolve}(a^2)) / (\\text{sum}(a))^2\\]\n\nWe validate against the official `evaluate_sequence` function.\n\n3. Implementation \nInitialize with the provided `height_sequence_1` or generate a new grid. Perform iterative upscaling and optimization. Key components include:\n\n- `propose_candidate`: Top-level function\n   1. Start with resolved grid fraction (support) and initial step-function\n   2. Loop until time budget exceeded\n   3. For each grid fraction (16, 8)-style, train a population with diffs\n   4. After training, repair the best candidate to satisfy the validator\n   5. Upsample if convergence is good or time permits\n\n- `repair_sequence(sequence)`: Adjusts the input to meet the validator's sum threshold and non-negativity\n\n- `upsample(a, factor=2.0)`: Increases sequence resolution to explore finer details\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nDefault parameters are:\n   - Dimension: 400\n   - Coefficient range: [0,1000]\n   - Grid fractions: [0.1, 0.05, 0.025]\n   - Crossover probability: 0.7\n   - Mutation factor: 0.1\n\nRepair is automatic if the sequence sum is below 0.01. Upscaling factor adaptive based on time remaining.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nOperations:\n   - Convolution: O(n log n) via FFT\n   - Genetic algorithm: Population size 40, time budget 900s\n\nSafety measures:\n   - Periodic validation checks against the official scorer\n   - Early termination if not improving\n   - Memory management: Grid ricing adapts to remaining time\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nAll sequences are validated via `evaluate_sequence`:\n   - Primary score is the official AC1 bound\n   - Fallback to initial solution if no valid sequence found\n   - Manual repair if NaN/Inf detected\n\nthoroughly documented and optimized for performance.",
